# Command abduco

## What does this command do:

abduco provides session management i.e. it allows programs to be run independently from its controlling terminal. That is programs can be detached - run in the background - and then later reattached. Together with dvtm it provides a simpler and cleaner alternative to tmux or screen.

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: sudo dnf install abduco
For Raspberry Pi install using: build from source (not in Debian repos)
  sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y build-essential git
  git clone https://github.com/martanne/abduco.git && cd abduco
  ./configure && make && sudo make install
For Mac install using: brew install abduco
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: sudo dnf install abduco

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/abduco
For Raspberry Pi run: /usr/local/bin/abduco
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/abduco
For Mac container run: /usr/bin/abduco

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://github.com/martanne/abduco

## Command options

# List all existing sessions
abduco

# Create a new session running <cmd> (detach with Ctrl-\)
abduco -c <session-name> <cmd>

# Create a new session using $ABDUCO_CMD or dvtm if unset
abduco -c <session-name>

# Attach to an existing session
abduco -a <session-name>

# Attach to a session read-only (keyboard input is ignored)
abduco -a -r <session-name>

# Create a new session but don't attach (run in background immediately)
abduco -n <session-name> <cmd>

# Set a custom detach character (default: Ctrl-\)
abduco -c <session-name> <cmd> -e '^q'

# Force attach even if another client is connected
abduco -A <session-name>

# Recreate a lost socket by sending SIGUSR1 to the server process
kill -USR1 $(pgrep -P 1 abduco)
